}", "recovery_added_pnums": []}, "results": {"selected_paragraphs_json": "[{"pnum": 20, "paragraph_text": "CONSORT flow diagram. Of 889 patients considered, 556 were judged not to require immediate antibiotics and were randomised. *Numbers based on general practitioner reports", "sentences": [{"sid": "S37", "text": "CONSORT flow diagram."}, {"sid": "S38", "text": "Of 889 patients considered, 556 were judged not to require immediate antibiotics and were randomised."}, {"sid": "S39", "text": "*Numbers based on general practitioner reports"}]}, {"pnum": 28, "paragraph_text": "A statistician independent of the study team coordinated the randomisation using computer generated random numbers. If the doctor thought that immediate antibiotics were definitely required, they prescribed antibiotics, otherwise patients were randomised to one of four methods of delayed antibiotic prescribing. We assessed evidence of subversion of randomisation by monitoring the order of use of envelopes (there was no evidence of selective envelope use) and the baseline table (which showed that the groups were well balanced).", "sentences": [{"sid": "S53", "text": "A statistician independent of the study team coordinated the randomisation using computer generated random numbers."}, {"sid": "S54", "text": "If the doctor thought that immediate antibiotics were definitely required, they prescribed antibiotics, otherwise patients were randomised to one of four methods of delayed antibiotic prescribing."}, {"sid": "S55", "text": "We assessed evidence of subversion of randomisation by monitoring the order of use of envelopes (there was no evidence of selective envelope use) and the baseline table (which showed that the groups were well balanced)."}]}, {"pnum": 29, "paragraph_text": "A key concern in complex pragmatic factorial trials is keeping the logistics simple and avoiding errors at the point of intervention delivery. We made it very simple to execute randomisation and delivery because the health professional took the next pack off the shelf that contained pre-randomised advice sheets (that is, there was no requirement—and hence no error—in finding and using the correct advice sheets). With careful attention to practitioner equipoise, this method of randomisation to different advice strategies has proved successful in previous trials.11 13 14 There was no evidence of subversion in the current study—either for selective envelope use or bias in patient characteristics.", "sentences": [{"sid": "S56", "text": "A key concern in complex pragmatic factorial trials is keeping the logistics simple and avoiding errors at the point of intervention delivery."}, {"sid": "S57", "text": "We made it very simple to execute randomisation and delivery because the health professional took the next pack off the shelf that contained pre-randomised advice sheets (that is, there was no requirement—and hence no error—in finding and using the correct advice sheets)."}, {"sid": "S58", "text": "With careful attention to practitioner equipoise, this method of randomisation to different advice strategies has proved successful in previous trials.11 13 14"}, {"sid": "S59", "text": "There was no evidence of subversion in the current study—either for selective envelope use or bias in patient characteristics."}]}, {"pnum": 49, "paragraph_text": "Results", "sentences": [{"sid": "S102", "text": "Results"}]}, {"pnum": 50, "paragraph_text": "A total of 889 patients were recruited between 3 March 2010 and 28 March 2012 by 53 health professionals in 25 practices. Of these patients, 333 (37%) were prescribed immediate antibiotics and 556 (63%) entered the randomised trial. Those prescribed immediate antibiotics had slightly more severe symptoms at baseline and were more likely to be labelled as having lower respiratory tract infections and less likely to be labelled as having upper respiratory tract infections. However, controlling for these characteristics did not alter the inferences of comparisons with the delayed and no prescription groups. Baseline characteristics were similar in all randomised groups (table1). All patients were followed up for one month; further follow-up after the initial month was a mean of 0.84 years (standard deviation 0.36) and was similar in all groups. The diary symptom severity scale had acceptable internal reliability for days 2-4 (Cronbach’s α, day 2, 0.72; day 3, 0.79; day 4, 0.79) and was sensitive to change over the week (standardised response mean 1.60). The non-recruitment questionnaire had responses from 20 respondents who recruited most of the patients (n=704). The most common reaso ...", "sentences": [{"sid": "S103", "text": "A total of 889 patients were recruited between 3 March 2010 and 28 March 2012 by 53 health professionals in 25 practices."}, {"sid": "S104", "text": "Of these patients, 333 (37%) were prescribed immediate antibiotics and 556 (63%) entered the randomised trial."}, {"sid": "S105", "text": "Those prescribed immediate antibiotics had slightly more severe symptoms at baseline and were more likely to be labelled as having lower respiratory tract infections and less likely to be labelled as having upper respiratory tract infections."}, {"sid": "S106", "text": "However, controlling for these characteristics did not alter the inferences of comparisons with the delayed and no prescription groups."}, {"sid": "S107", "text": "Baseline characteristics were similar in all randomised groups (table1)."}, {"sid": "S108", "text": "All patients were followed up for one month; further follow-up after the initial month was a mean of 0.84 years (standard deviation 0.36) and was similar in all groups."}, {"sid": "S109", "text": "The diary symptom severity scale had acceptable internal reliability for days 2-4 (Cronbach’s α, day 2, 0.72; day 3, 0.79; day 4, 0.79) and was sensitive to change over the week (standardised response mean 1.60)."}, {"sid": "S110", "text": "The non-recruitment questionnaire had responses from 20 respondents who recruited most of the patients (n=704)."}, {"sid": "S111", "text": "The most common reason (given by 14 recruiters) for eligible patients declining was that the patient was too busy or insufficiently interested, followed by concern about the proposed treatment, and the patient being too unwell."}, {"sid": "S112", "text": "The most common reason given (by 18 of 19 recruiters) for not approaching eligible patients was insufficient time."}]}, {"pnum": 51, "paragraph_text": "Table 1 Baseline characteristic of groups", "sentences": [{"sid": "S113", "text": "Table 1 Baseline characteristic of groups"}]}, {"pnum": 52, "paragraph_text": "Attrition bias", "sentences": [{"sid": "S114", "text": "Attrition bias"}]}, {"pnum": 53, "paragraph_text": "The characteristics of patients where the primary outcome was not documented were similar to those followed up (n=751) for baseline symptom score (1.0 v 0.98), female sex (82/136 (60%) v 458/744 (62%)), age 16 years or less (31/136 (23%) v 200/744 (27%)). The two groups were similar in diagnosis of lower respiratory tract infections (22/135 (16%) v 113/742 (15%)) and otitis media (16/134 (12%) v 66/740 (9%)).", "sentences": [{"sid": "S115", "text": "The characteristics of patients where the primary outcome was not documented were similar to those followed up (n=751) for baseline symptom score (1.0 v 0.98), female sex (82/136 (60%) v 458/744 (62%)), age 16 years or less (31/136 (23%) v 200/744 (27%))."}, {"sid": "S116", "text": "The two groups were similar in diagnosis of lower respiratory tract infections (22/135 (16%) v 113/742 (15%)) and otitis media (16/134 (12%) v 66/740 (9%))."}]}, {"pnum": 54, "paragraph_text": "Performance bias: compliance with delaying antibiotics", "sentences": [{"sid": "S117", "text": "Performance bias: compliance with delaying antibiotics"}]}, {"pnum": 55, "paragraph_text": "For the 264 patients who completed the main diary and who documented taking antibiotics, the median day that antibiotics were started was day 4 for all the delayed prescription strategies and day 1 for the immediate prescription strategy.", "sentences": [{"sid": "S118", "text": "For the 264 patients who completed the main diary and who documented taking antibiotics, the median day that antibiotics were started was day 4 for all the delayed prescription strategies and day 1 for the immediate prescription strategy."}]}, {"pnum": 56, "paragraph_text": "Outcomes", "sentences": [{"sid": "S119", "text": "Outcomes"}]}, {"pnum": 57, "paragraph_text": "For symptom severity, we saw no evidence of a significant interaction between antibiotic strategy and analgesia use (likelihood ratio test χ2 7.7, P=0.47; web appendix 4), nor dosing (4.1, P=0.39) or steam (5.3, P=0.26). There was also no evidence that the effect of antibiotic strategy differed after adaptation of the trial to include a no prescription arm, for either symptom severity (3.20, P=0.362) or antibiotic use (1.25, P=0.740).", "sentences": [{"sid": "S120", "text": "For symptom severity, we saw no evidence of a significant interaction between antibiotic strategy and analgesia use (likelihood ratio test χ2 7.7, P=0.47; web appendix 4), nor dosing (4.1, P=0.39) or steam (5.3, P=0.26)."}, {"sid": "S121", "text": "There was also no evidence that the effect of antibiotic strategy differed after adaptation of the trial to include a no prescription arm, for either symptom severity (3.20, P=0.362) or antibiotic use (1.25, P=0.740)."}]}, {"pnum": 58, "paragraph_text": "Main results", "sentences": [{"sid": "S122", "text": "Main results"}]}, {"pnum": 59, "paragraph_text": "In the randomised groups (no prescription and delayed prescription strategies), there was no significant effect of strategy on symptom severity (likelihood ratio test χ2 2.61, P=0.625), duration (4.29, P=0.368), and small differences of 0.1°C in temperature control (10.37, P=0.035; table 2 shows individual group comparisons). Antibiotic use did not differ significantly between strategies (4.96, P=0.292), with the lowest use (26%; 26/99) reported in the no prescription arm, an average 37% (134/367) in the delayed arms (varying from 33% (28/85) in the collection arm to 39% (35/89) in the patient led arm). Belief in the effectiveness of antibiotics was strong but not significantly different between groups (1.62, P=0.805). Higher levels of satisfaction were reported for the patient led and collection approaches, although the limited sample size for this outcome resulted in no significant differences overall (2.38, P=0.667). Reconsultations in the following month were similar (2.97, P=0.563) and were not significantly different after the first month (4.11, P=0.391).", "sentences": [{"sid": "S123", "text": "In the randomised groups (no prescription and delayed prescription strategies), there was no significant effect of strategy on symptom severity (likelihood ratio test χ2 2.61, P=0.625), duration (4.29, P=0.368), and small differences of 0.1°C in temperature control (10.37, P=0.035; table 2 shows individual group comparisons)."}, {"sid": "S124", "text": "Antibiotic use did not differ significantly between strategies (4.96, P=0.292), with the lowest use (26%; 26/99) reported in the no prescription arm, an average 37% (134/367) in the delayed arms (varying from 33% (28/85) in the collection arm to 39% (35/89) in the patient led arm)."}, {"sid": "S125", "text": "Belief in the effectiveness of antibiotics was strong but not significantly different between groups (1.62, P=0.805)."}, {"sid": "S126", "text": "Higher levels of satisfaction were reported for the patient led and collection approaches, although the limited sample size for this outcome resulted in no significant differences overall (2.38, P=0.667)."}, {"sid": "S127", "text": "Reconsultations in the following month were similar (2.97, P=0.563) and were not significantly different after the first month (4.11, P=0.391)."}]}, {"pnum": 60, "paragraph_text": "Table 2 Effectiveness of antibiotic strategies (randomised groups only)", "sentences": [{"sid": "S128", "text": "Table 2 Effectiveness of antibiotic strategies (randomised groups only)"}]}, {"pnum": 61, "paragraph_text": "After including the non-randomised immediate prescription group, there was no significant effect of antibiotic prescribing strategy on symptom severity (likelihood ratio test, χ2 4.05, P=0.543), duration (4.94, P=0.424), or temperature (7.66, P=0.176; web appendix 2 shows individual group comparisons). Antibiotic use differed significantly (326, P<0.001) with 97% (270/278) of patients reporting antibiotic use in the immediate arm, and more patients believed antibiotics were very effective (93% (168/180); 36.4, P<0.01) despite immediate antibiotics having no effect on symptom control or duration.", "sentences": [{"sid": "S129", "text": "After including the non-randomised immediate prescription group, there was no significant effect of antibiotic prescribing strategy on symptom severity (likelihood ratio test, χ2 4.05, P=0.543), duration (4.94, P=0.424), or temperature (7.66, P=0.176; web appendix 2 shows individual group comparisons)."}, {"sid": "S130", "text": "Antibiotic use differed significantly (326, P<0.001) with 97% (270/278) of patients reporting antibiotic use in the immediate arm, and more patients believed antibiotics were very effective (93% (168/180); 36.4, P<0.01) despite immediate antibiotics having no effect on symptom control or duration."}]}, {"pnum": 62, "paragraph_text": "Harms", "sentences": [{"sid": "S131", "text": "Harms"}]}, {"pnum": 63, "paragraph_text": "Complications were slightly more common in the no prescription group (3/122 (2.5%)) than in the delayed strategy groups (average 6/432 (1.4%)) and similar to the immediate group 8/326 (2.5%; web appendix 3). In multivariate analysis controlling for baseline symptoms, smoking, and diagnostic group, there were fewer complications in both the delayed and immediate groups but this difference was not significant (adjusted risk ratio 0.56 (95% confidence interval 0.13 to 2.37); 0.66 (0.15 to 2.88)). We saw little difference in other side effects reported between groups (web appendix 3).", "sentences": [{"sid": "S132", "text": "Complications were slightly more common in the no prescription group (3/122 (2.5%)) than in the delayed strategy groups (average 6/432 (1.4%)) and similar to the immediate group 8/326 (2.5%; web appendix 3)."}, {"sid": "S133", "text": "In multivariate analysis controlling for baseline symptoms, smoking, and diagnostic group, there were fewer complications in both the delayed and immediate groups but this difference was not significant (adjusted risk ratio 0.56 (95% confidence interval 0.13 to 2.37); 0.66 (0.15 to 2.88))."}, {"sid": "S134", "text": "We saw little difference in other side effects reported between groups (web appendix 3)."}]}]", "extraction_output_json": "{"article_id":"PMC3944682","section":"results","extractions":[{"item":"13a","pnum":20,"sid":"S37","score":0.9,"rationale":"The caption explicitly identifies a CONSORT participant flow diagram."},{"item":"13a","pnum":20,"sid":"S38","score":0.91,"rationale":"The sentence reports the number of considered patients who entered randomisation."},{"item":"14a","pnum":50,"sid":"S103","score":0.98,"rationale":"The sentence gives the recruitment period from 3 March 2010 to 28 March 2012."},{"item":"13a","pnum":50,"sid":"S104","score":0.88,"rationale":"The sentence accounts for patients prescribed immediate antibiotics and those entering the randomised trial."},{"item":"15","pnum":50,"sid":"S105","score":0.83,"rationale":"The sentence describes baseline symptom severity and respiratory-infection diagnoses for participant groups."},{"item":"18","pnum":50,"sid":"S106","score":0.76,"rationale":"The sentence reports that adjusted comparisons controlling for baseline characteristics did not change inferences."},{"item":"15","pnum":50,"sid":"S107","score":0.94,"rationale":"The sentence reports baseline