}", "recovery_added_pnums": []}, "results": {"selected_paragraphs_json": "[{"pnum": 28, "paragraph_text": "Results", "sentences": [{"sid": "S78", "text": "Results"}]}, {"pnum": 29, "paragraph_text": "We evaluated 782 patients with clinically significant MRSA isolates, of whom 252 were included. The main reasons for exclusion were previous treatment with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole or vancomycin for more than 48 hours or inability to provide informed consent, related mostly to severity of sepsis (figure). Inclusion was based on microbiologically documented inclusion criteria in 245 (97%) patients: 91 (36%) with bacteraemia and 154 (61%) with MRSA isolated from other clinical samples. Patients’ mean age was 65.8 (SD 17) years, and all infections were healthcare associated (218 (87%) hospital acquired). Characteristics of patients and infections were mostly balanced between groups; the exception was bacteraemia, which was more common in the vancomycin group (table 1). Appropriate empirical antibiotic treatment was infrequent. Surgical interventions and catheter extraction, when indicated, were performed in similar proportions in the two study groups. Vancomycin trough concentrations were available for 97/117 (82.9%) patients in the vancomycin arm (≥10 µg/mL in 80 (83%) patients and ≥15 µg/mL in 65 (67%) patients). Isolates’ minimum inhibitory concentration to vancomycin was 2 µg ...", "sentences": [{"sid": "S79", "text": "We evaluated 782 patients with clinically significant MRSA isolates, of whom 252 were included."}, {"sid": "S80", "text": "The main reasons for exclusion were previous treatment with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole or vancomycin for more than 48 hours or inability to provide informed consent, related mostly to severity of sepsis (figure)."}, {"sid": "S81", "text": "Inclusion was based on microbiologically documented inclusion criteria in 245 (97%) patients: 91 (36%) with bacteraemia and 154 (61%) with MRSA isolated from other clinical samples."}, {"sid": "S82", "text": "Patients’ mean age was 65.8 (SD 17) years, and all infections were healthcare associated (218 (87%) hospital acquired)."}, {"sid": "S83", "text": "Characteristics of patients and infections were mostly balanced between groups; the exception was bacteraemia, which was more common in the vancomycin group (table 1)."}, {"sid": "S84", "text": "Appropriate empirical antibiotic treatment was infrequent."}, {"sid": "S85", "text": "Surgical interventions and catheter extraction, when indicated, were performed in similar proportions in the two study groups."}, {"sid": "S86", "text": "Vancomycin trough concentrations were available for 97/117 (82.9%) patients in the vancomycin arm (≥10 µg/mL in 80 (83%) patients and ≥15 µg/mL in 65 (67%) patients)."}, {"sid": "S87", "text": "Isolates’ minimum inhibitory concentration to vancomycin was 2 µg/mL in 12/77 (16%) of patients in the vancomycin arm and lower in the remaining patients."}, {"sid": "S88", "text": "All isolates were susceptible to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (disk zone ≤10 mm)."}, {"sid": "S89", "text": "The median duration of treatment with assigned antibiotics in the per protocol population was 17 (95% confidence interval 12 to 22) days with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (n=110) and 14 (13 to 15) days with vancomycin (n=96)."}, {"sid": "S90", "text": "Potentially effective antibiotics against MRSA (mainly rifampin) were added throughout the treatment course for 14/135 (10%) patients in the trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole arm and 8/117 (7%) patients in the vancomycin arm (P=0.32)."}]}, {"pnum": 30, "paragraph_text": "Flow of patients through study. BMT=bone marrow transplant; MRSA=meticillin resistant Staphylococcus aureus", "sentences": [{"sid": "S91", "text": "Flow of patients through study."}, {"sid": "S92", "text": "BMT=bone marrow transplant; MRSA=meticillin resistant Staphylococcus aureus"}]}, {"pnum": 31, "paragraph_text": "Table 1 Patients’ characteristics. Values are numbers (percentages) unless stated otherwise", "sentences": [{"sid": "S93", "text": "Table 1 Patients’ characteristics."}, {"sid": "S94", "text": "Values are numbers (percentages) unless stated otherwise"}]}, {"pnum": 32, "paragraph_text": "We found no significant difference in treatment failure at day 7 between trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and vancomycin (risk ratio 1.38, 95% confidence interval 0.96 to 1.99) (table 2. The failure rate with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole was 51/135 (38%) compared with 32/117 (27%) with vancomycin, and the 95% confidence interval for the difference fell outside the lower limit of the 15% predefined for non-inferiority (−1.2% to 21.5%). Of the components comprising the composite outcome, the advantage to vancomycin emerged from higher bacteraemia persistence at day 7 and lack of improvement in SOFA score at day 7 with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. We observed similar results in the per protocol population of patients completing seven days’ treatment (risk ratio 1.24, 0.82 to 1.89; absolute difference 9.9%, −3.1% to 22.5%) and for patients with bacteraemia (risk ratio 1.40, 0.91 to 2.16). Restricting the analysis to patients in the vancomycin group whose isolates’ minimum inhibitory concentrations were below 2 µg/mL resulted in a larger risk ratio in favour of vancomycin (1.64, 0.99 to 2.68).", "sentences": [{"sid": "S95", "text": "We found no significant difference in treatment failure at day 7 between trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and vancomycin (risk ratio 1.38, 95% confidence interval 0.96 to 1.99) (table 2."}, {"sid": "S96", "text": "The failure rate with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole was 51/135 (38%) compared with 32/117 (27%) with vancomycin, and the 95% confidence interval for the difference fell outside the lower limit of the 15% predefined for non-inferiority (−1.2% to 21.5%)."}, {"sid": "S97", "text": "Of the components comprising the composite outcome, the advantage to vancomycin emerged from higher bacteraemia persistence at day 7 and lack of improvement in SOFA score at day 7 with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole."}, {"sid": "S98", "text": "We observed similar results in the per protocol population of patients completing seven days’ treatment (risk ratio 1.24, 0.82 to 1.89; absolute difference 9.9%, −3.1% to 22.5%) and for patients with bacteraemia (risk ratio 1.40, 0.91 to 2.16)."}, {"sid": "S99", "text": "Restricting the analysis to patients in the vancomycin group whose isolates’ minimum inhibitory concentrations were below 2 µg/mL resulted in a larger risk ratio in favour of vancomycin (1.64, 0.99 to 2.68)."}]}, {"pnum": 33, "paragraph_text": "Table 2 Study outcomes. Values are numbers (percentages) unless stated otherwise", "sentences": [{"sid": "S100", "text": "Table 2 Study outcomes."}, {"sid": "S101", "text": "Values are numbers (percentages) unless stated otherwise"}]}, {"pnum": 34, "paragraph_text": "All cause 30 day mortality did not differ significantly between groups. However, among patients with bacteraemia, mortality was nearly twice as high with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole—14/41 (34%) versus 9/50 (18%) with vancomycin (risk ratio 1.90, 0.92 to 3.93). Among non-bacteraemic patients, mortality rates were low (5/94 (5%) v 4/67 (6%)) and not significantly different (risk ratio 0.89, 0.25 to 3.2).", "sentences": [{"sid": "S102", "text": "All cause 30 day mortality did not differ significantly between groups."}, {"sid": "S103", "text": "However, among patients with bacteraemia, mortality was nearly twice as high with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole—14/41 (34%) versus 9/50 (18%) with vancomycin (risk ratio 1.90, 0.92 to 3.93)."}, {"sid": "S104", "text": "Among non-bacteraemic patients, mortality rates were low (5/94 (5%) v 4/67 (6%)) and not significantly different (risk ratio 0.89, 0.25 to 3.2)."}]}, {"pnum": 35, "paragraph_text": "We found no significant differences with regard to the pre-defined secondary outcomes (table 2). Persistence of bacteraemia was slightly more common with vancomycin at 48 hours and with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole at seven days. There was no significant difference in length of hospital admission for patients discharged alive. Adverse events were reported with similar frequency. Renal failure at day 7 and day 30 was slightly more common with vancomycin, but this was not statistically significant (table 3).", "sentences": [{"sid": "S105", "text": "We found no significant differences with regard to the pre-defined secondary outcomes (table 2)."}, {"sid": "S106", "text": "Persistence of bacteraemia was slightly more common with vancomycin at 48 hours and with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole at seven days."}, {"sid": "S107", "text": "There was no significant difference in length of hospital admission for patients discharged alive."}, {"sid": "S108", "text": "Adverse events were reported with similar frequency."}, {"sid": "S109", "text": "Renal failure at day 7 and day 30 was slightly more common with vancomycin, but this was not statistically significant (table 3)."}]}, {"pnum": 36, "paragraph_text": "Table 3 Adverse events. Values are numbers (percentages) unless stated otherwise", "sentences": [{"sid": "S110", "text": "Table 3 Adverse events."}, {"sid": "S111", "text": "Values are numbers (percentages) unless stated otherwise"}]}, {"pnum": 37, "paragraph_text": "Variables significantly associated with the primary outcome on univariate analysis were included in the multivariable analysis. McCabe score, presence of nasogastric tube or urine catheter at onset of infection, white blood count, and albumin concentrations were associated with treatment failure but correlated with other included variables and were not entered into the regression analysis. Other significant variables are shown in table 4. On multivariable analysis, allocation to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole was significantly associated with treatment failure (adjusted odds ratio 2.00, 1.09 to 3.65). Other independent risk factors were bacteraemia and mechanical ventilation at infection onset; surgery in the 30 days before infection was inversely associated with treatment failure.", "sentences": [{"sid": "S112", "text": "Variables significantly associated with the primary outcome on univariate analysis were included in the multivariable analysis."}, {"sid": "S113", "text": "McCabe score, presence of nasogastric tube or urine catheter at onset of infection, white blood count, and albumin concentrations were associated with treatment failure but correlated with other included variables and were not entered into the regression analysis."}, {"sid": "S114", "text": "Other significant variables are shown in table 4."}, {"sid": "S115", "text": "On multivariable analysis, allocation to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole was significantly associated with treatment failure (adjusted odds ratio 2.00, 1.09 to 3.65)."}, {"sid": "S116", "text": "Other independent risk factors were bacteraemia and mechanical ventilation at infection onset; surgery in the 30 days before infection was inversely associated with treatment failure."}]}, {"pnum": 38, "paragraph_text": "Table 4 Multivariate analysis for treatment failure", "sentences": [{"sid": "S117", "text": "Table 4 Multivariate analysis for treatment failure"}]}]", "extraction_output_json": "{"article_id":"PMC4431679","section":"results","extractions":[{"item":"13a","pnum":29,"sid":"S79","score":0.84,"rationale":"The sentence reports the number of evaluated patients and those included in the trial."},{"item":"15","pnum":29,"sid":"S81","score":0.78,"rationale":"The sentence describes microbiological and bacteraemia characteristics of included patients."},{"item":"15","pnum":29,"sid":"S82","score":0.84,"rationale":"The sentence reports participant age and healthcare-associated infection characteristics."},{"item":"15","pnum":29,"sid":"S83","score":0.94,"rationale":"The sentence compares patient and infection characteristics between groups and identifies bacteraemia imbalance."},{"item":"16","pnum":29,"sid":"S89","score":0.89,"rationale":"The sentence identifies per-protocol population sizes for both treatment groups."},{"item":"13a","pnum":30,"sid":"S91","score":0.92,"rationale":"The caption explicitly identifies a display showing patient flow through the study."},{"item":"15","pnum":31,"sid":"S93","score":0.93,"rationale":"The table caption identifies patient characteristics for the study groups."},{"item":"17a","pnum":32,"sid":"S95","score":0.97,"rationale":"The sentence reports the primary day-7 treatment-failure comparison with a risk ratio and confidence interval."},{"item":"17b","pnum":32,"sid":"S95","score":0.87,"rationale":"The sentence reports a relative effect estimate for the binary treatment-failure outcome."},{"item":"17a","pnum":32,"sid":"S96","score":0.99,"rationale":"The sentence gives arm-specific treatment-failure counts and percentages with the absolute confidence interval."},{"item":"17b","pnum":32,"sid":"S96","score":0.97,"rationale":"The sentence presents absolute binary failure frequencies by group and an absolute difference interval."},{"item":"17a","pnum":32,"sid":"S97","score":0.90,"rationale":"The sentence reports comparative findings for components of the composite primary outcome."},{"item":"17a","pnum":32,"sid":"S98","score":0.94,"rationale":"The sentence reports per-protocol and bacteraemia-subgroup treatment-failure effect estimates."},{"item":"18","pnum":32,"sid":"S98","score":0.94,"rationale":"The sentence reports distinct per-protocol and bacteraemia subgroup analyses."},{"item":"17a","pnum":32,"sid":"S99","score":0.90,"rationale":"The sentence reports a treatment-failure risk ratio in the restricted susceptibility analysis."},{"item":"18","pnum":32,"sid":"S99","score":0.94,"rationale":"The sentence reports a restricted analysis based on vancomycin isolate susceptibility."},{"item":"17a","pnum":33,"sid":"S100","score":0.87,"rationale":"The caption identifies a table presenting study outcome results."},{"item":"17a","pnum":34,"sid":"S102","score":0.91,"rationale":"The sentence reports the between-group result for 30-day all-cause mortality."},{"item":"17a","pnum":34,"sid":"S103","score":0.98,"rationale":"The sentence reports bacteraemia-subgroup mortality counts, percentages, and a risk ratio."},{"item":"17b","pnum":34,"sid":"S103","score":0.97,"rationale":"The sentence gives binary mortality frequencies by arm and a relative risk estimate."},{"item":"18","pnum":34,"sid":"S103","score":0.91,"rationale":"The sentence reports mortality results for the bacteraemia subgroup."},{"item":"17a","pnum":34,"sid":"S104","score":0.95,"rationale":"The sentence reports non-bacteraemic subgroup mortality rates and a risk ratio."},{"item":"17b","pnum":34,"sid":"S104","score":0.94,"rationale":"The sentence provides binary mortality frequencies by arm with a relative risk estimate."},{"item":"18","pnum":34,"sid":"S104","score":0.89,"rationale":"The sentence reports mortality findings in the non-bacteraemic subgroup."},{"item":"17a","pnum":35,"sid":"S105","score":0.89,"rationale":"The sentence reports the overall result for predefined secondary outcomes."},{"item":"17a","pnum":35,"sid":"S106","score":0.88,"rationale":"The sentence reports comparative persistence-of-bacteraemia findings at two timepoints."},{"item":"17a","pnum":35,"sid":"S107","score":0.87,"rationale":"The sentence reports the comparative result for length of hospital admission."},{"item":"17a","pnum":35,"sid":"S108","score":0.86,"rationale":"The sentence reports the comparative frequency finding for adverse events."},{"item":"19","pnum":35,"sid":"S108","score":0.96,"rationale":"The sentence reports adverse events occurring with similar frequency between groups."},{"item":"17a","pnum":35,"sid":"S109","score":0.89,"rationale":"The sentence reports the comparative renal-failure result at days 7 and 30."},{"item":"19","pnum":35,"sid":"S109","score":0.94,"rationale":"The sentence reports renal failure as a safety-related adverse outcome."},{"item":"19","pnum":36,"sid":"S110","score":0.96,"rationale":"The table caption explicitly identifies adverse-event results."},{"item":"18","pnum":37,"sid":"S113","score":0.86,"rationale":"The sentence reports univariate associations between clinical variables and treatment failure."},{"item":"17a","pnum":37,"sid":"S115","score":0.94,"rationale":"The sentence reports the adjusted association of treatment allocation with treatment failure."},{"item":"18","pnum":37,"sid":"S115","score":0.95,"rationale":"The sentence reports an adjusted multivariable analysis result."},{"item":"18","pnum":37,"sid":"S116","score":0.90,"rationale":"The sentence reports independent risk-factor findings from multivariable analysis."},{"item":"18","pnum":38,"sid":"S117","score":0.88,"rationale":"The table caption identifies a multivariate analysis of treatment failure."}]}", "recovery_added_pnums": []}, "discussion": {"selected_paragraphs_json": "[{"pnum": 39, "paragraph_text": "Discussion", "sentences": [{"sid": "S118", "text": "Discussion"}]}, {"pnum": 40, "paragraph_text": "In a randomised controlled trial including 252 patients with severe MRSA infections, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole did not fulfil criteria for non-inferiority to vancomycin. The absolute difference in treatment failure rates at day 7—comprising death, clinical/haemodynamic instability, and persistence of bacteraemia—was 10.4% (−1.2% to 21.5%) in favour of vancomycin, crossing the upper limit of 15% difference defined for non-inferiority. After adjustment for differences between groups, treatment with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole was significantly associated with treatment failure (odds ratio 2.00, 1.09 to 3.65). Thirty day mortality did not differ significantly between groups (risk ratio 1.27, 0.65 to 2.45); however, among patients with bacteraemia (n=91), death rates were non-significantly higher with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. Results were similar in a per protocol analysis. Bacteriological cure and adverse event rates did not differ significantly between groups.", "sentences": [{"sid": "S119", "text": "In a randomised controlled trial including 252 patients with severe MRSA infections, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole did not fulfil criteria for non-inferiority to vancomycin."}, {"sid": "S120", "text": "The absolute difference in treatment failure rates at day 7—comprising death, clinical/haemodynamic instability, and persistence of bacteraemia—was 10.4% (−1.2% to 21.5%) in favour of vancomycin, crossing the upper limit of 15% difference defined for non-inferiority."}, {"sid": "S121", "text": "After adjustment for differences between groups, treatment with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole was significantly associated with treatment failure (odds ratio 2.00, 1.09 to 3.65)."}, {"sid": "S122", "text": "Thirty day mortality did not differ significantly between groups (risk ratio 1.27, 0.65 to 2.45); however, among patients with bacteraemia (n=91), death rates were non-significantly higher with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole."}, {"sid": "S123", "text": "Results were similar in a per protocol analysis."}, {"sid": "S124", "text": "Bacteriological cure and adverse event rates did not differ significantly between groups."}]}, {"pnum": 41, "paragraph_text": "Our trial was pragmatic, targeting all patients treated with vancomycin in clinical practice. We defined no exclusion criteria related to severity of background illness or sepsis. Exclusions were based only on contraindications for trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (such as chronic haemodialysis) or previous evidence suggesting inferiority of trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. We thus excluded left sided endocarditis owing to an in vivo study of endocarditis in rabbits showing lower survival and vegetation sterilisation and higher bacterial load on vegetations with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole compared with vancomycin and other antibiotics.32 We excluded patients with MRSA meningitis owing to inferiority of trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole to nafcillin in an experimental model of meticillin susceptible S aureus meningitis and lack of evidence in MRSA, despite good penetration of trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole to the brain.33 34 We originally intended to recruit patients with non-microbiologically documented, highly probable MRSA infections. However, in practice, we found identifying such patients prospectively difficult and practically the trial included only patients with microbiologically c ...", "sentences": [{"sid": "S125", "text": "Our trial was pragmatic, targeting all patients treated with vancomycin in clinical practice."}, {"sid": "S126", "text": "We defined no exclusion criteria related to severity of background illness or sepsis."}, {"sid": "S127", "text": "Exclusions were based only on contraindications for trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (such as chronic haemodialysis) or previous evidence suggesting inferiority of trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole."}, {"sid": "S128", "text": "We thus excluded left sided endocarditis owing to an in vivo study of endocarditis in rabbits showing lower survival and vegetation sterilisation and higher bacterial load on vegetations with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole compared with vancomycin and other antibiotics.32"}, {"sid": "S129", "text": "We excluded patients with MRSA meningitis owing to inferiority of trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole to nafcillin in an experimental model of meticillin susceptible S aureus meningitis and lack of evidence in MRSA, despite good penetration of trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole to the brain.33 34"}, {"sid": "S130", "text": "We originally intended to recruit patients with non-microbiologically documented, highly probable MRSA infections."}, {"sid": "S131", "text": "However, in practice, we found identifying such patients prospectively difficult and practically the trial included only patients with microbiologically confirmed MRSA infections."}, {"sid": "S132", "text": "The main reason precluding inclusion of patients was inability to obtain informed consent at the time of acute sepsis."}, {"sid": "S133", "text": "In Israel, regulations allow only legal guardians to replace patients’ informed consent (informed consent from relatives is not allowed), and with acute conditions most patients did not have an appointed legal guardian."}]}, {"pnum": 42, "paragraph_text": "Comparison with other studies", "sentences": [{"sid": "S134", "text": "Comparison with other studies"}]}, {"pnum": 43, "paragraph_text": "Few randomised controlled trials have assessed the effectiveness of trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for the treatment of staphylococcal infections. A single randomised controlled trial compared trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole against vancomycin for staphylococcal infections.23 The trial randomised 228 intravenous drug users with suspected staphylococcal infections and analysed 101 with proven infections (54 with meticillin susceptible S aureus and 47 with MRSA). Vancomycin was superior to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole among patients with meticillin susceptible S aureus infections, but no differences were observed for patients with MRSA. Specifically, bacteraemia duration (total 57 patients) was longer with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole treatment for meticillin susceptible S aureus (7.8 v 3.5 days; P=0.05) and similar for MRSA (5.2 v 5.6 days). The authors concluded that trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is a valuable alternative for the treatment of MRSA infections, unlike the conclusions of our study. However, the study included significantly younger and less ill patients, with a single death compared with the 13% mortality rate in our study. In a recent randomised controlled trial, trime ...", "sentences": [{"sid": "S135", "text": "Few randomised controlled trials have assessed the effectiveness of trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for the treatment of staphylococcal infections."}, {"sid": "S136", "text": "A single randomised controlled trial compared trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole against vancomycin for staphylococcal infections.23"}, {"sid": "S137", "text": "The trial randomised 228 intravenous drug users with suspected staphylococcal infections and analysed 101 with proven infections (54 with meticillin susceptible S aureus and 47 with MRSA)."}, {"sid": "S138", "text": "Vancomycin was superior to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole among patients with meticillin susceptible S aureus infections, but no differences were observed for patients with MRSA."}, {"sid": "S139", "text": "Specifically, bacteraemia duration (total 57 patients) was longer with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole treatment for meticillin susceptible S aureus (7.8 v 3.5 days; P=0.05) and similar for MRSA (5.2 v 5.6 days)."}, {"sid": "S140", "text": "The authors concluded that trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is a valuable alternative for the treatment of MRSA infections, unlike the conclusions of our study."}, {"sid": "S141", "text": "However, the study included significantly younger and less ill patients, with a single death compared with the 13% mortality rate in our study."}, {"sid": "S142", "text": "In a recent randomised controlled trial, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole combined with rifampin was compared with linezolid in the treatment of MRSA infections (bacteraemia in 18/150 (12%) and mortality in 14/150 (9%)), showing no differences in failure/relapse rates or mortality.15"}, {"sid": "S143", "text": "Among 50 patients with chronic meticillin susceptible S aureus osteomyelitis randomised to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole-rifampin versus oxacillin, long term outcomes were similar and patients allocated to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole-rifampin had a significantly shorter hospital stay (median 51 v 31 days).35"}, {"sid": "S144", "text": "Finally, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole was not significantly different from doxycycline for uncomplicated skin and soft tissue infections in a small randomised controlled trial assessing 22 patients with MRSA.36"}]}, {"pnum": 44, "paragraph_text": "The main treatment options for MRSA infections other than vancomycin (or teicoplanin) and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole include linezolid, tedizolid, telavancin, dalbavancin, oritavancin, daptomycin, tigecycline, and ceftaroline.37 Most of the new antibiotics were evaluated for the treatment of skin and soft tissue infections in randomised controlled trials. Mortality was rarely assessed in these trials and when reported was very low (usually <1%). Few randomised controlled trials targeted infections other than skin and soft tissue infections, and most did not include only MRSA. Daptomycin was non-inferior to standard therapy in a randomised controlled trial including 245 patients with S aureus bacteraemia, of whom 89 had MRSA bacteraemia and were treated with daptomycin or vancomycin.38 The overall mortality was 11%. Similar efficacy and safety for was shown for linezolid and vancomycin in nosocomial pneumonia, and the mortality rate in these trials was 14%.39 Tigecycline was non-inferior to vancomycin in a randomised controlled trial including 156 patients with invasive MRSA infections, but most were skin and soft tissue infections (mortality rate 5%).40 Telavancin was non-inferi ...", "sentences": [{"sid": "S145", "text": "The main treatment options for MRSA infections other than vancomycin (or teicoplanin) and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole include linezolid, tedizolid, telavancin, dalbavancin, oritavancin, daptomycin, tigecycline, and ceftaroline.37"}, {"sid": "S146", "text": "Most of the new antibiotics were evaluated for the treatment of skin and soft tissue infections in randomised controlled trials."}, {"sid": "S147", "text": "Mortality was rarely assessed in these trials and when reported was very low (usually <1%)."}, {"sid": "S148", "text": "Few randomised controlled trials targeted infections other than skin and soft tissue infections, and most did not include only MRSA."}, {"sid": "S149", "text": "Daptomycin was non-inferior to standard therapy in a randomised controlled trial including 245 patients with S aureus bacteraemia, of whom 89 had MRSA bacteraemia and were treated with daptomycin or vancomycin.38"}, {"sid": "S150", "text": "The overall mortality was 11%."}, {"sid": "S151", "text": "Similar efficacy and safety for was shown for linezolid and vancomycin in nosocomial pneumonia, and the mortality rate in these trials was 14%.39"}, {"sid": "S152", "text": "Tigecycline was non-inferior to vancomycin in a randomised controlled trial including 156 patients with invasive MRSA infections, but most were skin and soft tissue infections (mortality rate 5%).40"}, {"sid": "S153", "text": "Telavancin was non-inferior to vancomycin in hospital acquired pneumonia (293 patients with MRSA, overall mortality in the study 19%).41"}, {"sid": "S154", "text": "Compared with these trials, ours included a larger group of patients with MRSA bacteraemia and a sicker cohort overall, as evidenced by the mortality rates, more closely reflecting patients treated with MRSA infections in clinical practice."}]}, {"pnum": 45, "paragraph_text": "Limitations of study", "sentences": [{"sid": "S155", "text": "Limitations of study"}]}, {"pnum": 46, "paragraph_text": "The main limitation of our trial is the small sample size of patients with bacteraemia, in whom results suggest an important advantage for vancomycin. No statistically significant differences were observed between groups at baseline for nearly all variables. However, a subtle difference existed for important prognostic variables, and more patients allocated to vancomycin were bacteraemic. Multivariable analysis of the entire study cohort showed a larger advantage of vancomycin with regard to treatment failure, reaching statistical significance. Patients with polymicrobial infections and receiving additional antibiotics were included in the study, reflecting its pragmatic nature. However, these were mostly inactive against MRSA and concomitant antibiotics covering MRSA were not allowed at randomisation. Patients excluded owing to inability to provide informed consent had higher severity of illness scores than those included, affecting the trial’s external validity. Finally, the dosing of vancomycin and trough levels achieved (≥15 µg/mL in only 67% of patients) were lower than currently recommended,20 potentially resulting in an underestimation of the efficacy of vancomycin and the d ...", "sentences": [{"sid": "S156", "text": "The main limitation of our trial is the small sample size of patients with bacteraemia, in whom results suggest an important advantage for vancomycin."}, {"sid": "S157", "text": "No statistically significant differences were observed between groups at baseline for nearly all variables."}, {"sid": "S158", "text": "However, a subtle difference existed for important prognostic variables, and more patients allocated to vancomycin were bacteraemic."}, {"sid": "S159", "text": "Multivariable analysis of the entire study cohort showed a larger advantage of vancomycin with regard to treatment failure, reaching statistical significance."}, {"sid": "S160", "text": "Patients with polymicrobial infections and receiving additional antibiotics were included in the study, reflecting its pragmatic nature."}, {"sid": "S161", "text": "However, these were mostly inactive against MRSA and concomitant antibiotics covering MRSA were not allowed at randomisation."}, {"sid": "S162", "text": "Patients excluded owing to inability to provide informed consent had higher severity of illness scores than those included, affecting the trial’s external validity."}, {"sid": "S163", "text": "Finally, the dosing of vancomycin and trough levels achieved (≥15 µg/mL in only 67% of patients) were lower than currently recommended,20 potentially resulting in an underestimation of the efficacy of vancomycin and the difference between arms."}]}, {"pnum": 47, "paragraph_text": "Conclusion", "sentences": [{"sid": "S164", "text": "Conclusion"}]}, {"pnum": 48, "paragraph_text": "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole did not achieve non-inferiority compared with vancomycin among patients with invasive MRSA infections. In the subgroup of patients with bacteraemia, the difference in treatment failure and all cause mortality might be clinically important. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole should not be used for the treatment of severe MRSA infections. We propose a further randomised controlled trial to examine the feasibility of step-down from vancomycin to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, allowing early discharge of patients with MRSA responding to treatment.", "sentences": [{"sid": "S165", "text": "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole did not achieve non-inferiority compared with vancomycin among patients with invasive MRSA infections."}, {"sid": "S166", "text": "In the subgroup of patients with bacteraemia, the difference in treatment failure and all cause mortality might be clinically important."}, {"sid": "S167", "text": "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole should not be used for the treatment of severe MRSA infections."}, {"sid": "S168", "text": "We propose a further randomised controlled trial to examine the feasibility of step-down from vancomycin to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, allowing early discharge of patients with MRSA responding to treatment."}]}, {"pnum": 53, "paragraph_text": "What this study adds", "sentences": [{"sid": "S173", "text": "What this study adds"}]}, {"pnum": 54, "paragraph_text": "In a randomised controlled trial including 252 inpatients, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole did not achieve non-inferiority to vancomycin in the treatment of severe MRSA infections", "sentences": [{"sid": "S174", "text": "In a randomised controlled trial including 252 inpatients, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole did not achieve non-inferiority to vancomycin in the treatment of severe MRSA infections"}]}, {"pnum": 55, "paragraph_text": "The difference was particularly marked for patients with bacteraemia", "sentences": [{"sid": "S175", "text": "The difference was particularly marked for patients with bacteraemia"}]}, {"pnum": 56, "paragraph_text": "Adverse events rates were similar for trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and vancomycin", "sentences": [{"sid": "S176", "text": "Adverse events rates were similar for trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and vancomycin"}]}]", "extraction_output_json": "{"article_id":"PMC4431679","section":"discussion","extractions":[{"item":"22","pnum":40,"sid":"S119","score":0.92,"rationale":"The sentence interprets the trial's primary comparison by concluding that trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole failed non-inferiority to vancomycin."},{"item":"22","pnum":40,"sid":"S123","score":0.85,"rationale":"The similar per-protocol findings support robustness of the trial's primary result."},{"item":"21","pnum":41,"sid":"S125","score":0.83,"rationale":"The trial is described as pragmatic and targeting patients treated with vancomycin in clinical practice, addressing applicability."},{"item":"22","pnum":43,"sid":"S140","score":0.86,"rationale":"The sentence contextualizes the current trial's conclusion by contrasting it with another trial's conclusion."},{"item":"21","pnum":44,"sid":"S154","score":0.88,"rationale":"The sentence argues that the trial's sicker cohort more closely reflects patients treated for MRSA in clinical practice."},{"item":"20","pnum":46,"sid":"S156","score":0.97,"rationale":"The sentence identifies the small bacteraemia subgroup sample as a trial limitation."},{"item":"20","pnum":46,"sid":"S158","score":0.84,"rationale":"The sentence identifies an imbalance in important prognostic variables and bacteraemia allocation that could bias comparisons."},{"item":"22","pnum":46,"sid":"S159","score":0.82,"rationale":"The multivariable analysis is used to interpret the treatment-failure comparison as showing a larger significant vancomycin advantage."},{"item":"20","pnum":46,"sid":"S162","score":0.94,"rationale":"Excluding patients unable to consent produced a less severely ill sample, limiting the evidence base."},{"item":"21","pnum":46,"sid":"S162","score":0.98,"rationale":"The sentence explicitly states that exclusion of sicker patients affected the trial's external validity."},{"item":"20","pnum":46,"sid":"S163","score":0.96,"rationale":"Lower-than-recommended vancomycin dosing and trough levels could underestimate vancomycin efficacy and the between-arm difference."},{"item":"22","pnum":48,"sid":"S165","score":0.94,"rationale":"The conclusion interprets the findings as failure of trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole to achieve non-inferiority."},{"item":"22","pnum":48,"sid":"S166","score":0.91,"rationale":"The sentence interprets subgroup differences in treatment failure and mortality as potentially clinically important."},{"item":"22","pnum":48,"sid":"S167","score":0.97,"rationale":"The sentence draws a treatment recommendation from the trial findings for severe MRSA infections."}]}", "recovery_added_pnums": []}, "other_information": {"selected_paragraphs_json": "[{"pnum": 11, "paragraph_text": "Trial registration Clinical trials NCT00427076.", "sentences": [{"sid": "S22", "text": "Trial registration Clinical trials NCT00427076."}]}, {"pnum": 21, "paragraph_text": "We used a central computer generated random number list to randomise patients to treatment with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole or vancomycin. Allocation was concealed in sealed, opaque numbered envelopes that were opened consecutively after informed consent was obtained. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole was started intravenously at a dose of 320 mg trimethoprim/1600 mg sulfamethoxazole twice daily and could be switched to oral treatment using the same dose at the discretion of the treating physician. Dosing was based on a pharmacokinetic study showing that a lower dose resulted in inhibition but not killing of S aureus and on the dose used in a previous trial.23 29 The vancomycin starting dose was 1 g twice daily. In both arms, treatment was adjusted to renal function; in the vancomycin arm, it was directed by serum concentrations to obtain drug trough concentrations between 10 and 20 mg/dL. The complete study protocol is available on request. Treatment had to be administered for a minimum of seven days, following which the duration depended on the indication. Concomitant antibiotics, other than the combination of vancomycin and co-trimoxazole, could be administered. After randomisatio ...", "sentences": [{"sid": "S50", "text": "We used a central computer generated random number list to randomise patients to treatment with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole or vancomycin."}, {"sid": "S51", "text": "Allocation was concealed in sealed, opaque numbered envelopes that were opened consecutively after informed consent was obtained."}, {"sid": "S52", "text": "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole was started intravenously at a dose of 320 mg trimethoprim/1600 mg sulfamethoxazole twice daily and could be switched to oral treatment using the same dose at the discretion of the treating physician."}, {"sid": "S53", "text": "Dosing was based on a pharmacokinetic study showing that a lower dose resulted in inhibition but not killing of S aureus and on the dose used in a previous trial.23 29"}, {"sid": "S54", "text": "The vancomycin starting dose was 1 g twice daily."}, {"sid": "S55", "text": "In both arms, treatment was adjusted to renal function; in the vancomycin arm, it was directed by serum concentrations to obtain drug trough concentrations between 10 and 20 mg/dL."}, {"sid": "S56", "text": "The complete study protocol is available on request."}, {"sid": "S57", "text": "Treatment had to be administered for a minimum of seven days, following which the duration depended on the indication."}, {"sid": "S58", "text": "Concomitant antibiotics, other than the combination of vancomycin and co-trimoxazole, could be administered."}, {"sid": "S59", "text": "After randomisation, no blinding was performed, but outcomes were adjudicated blinded to allocation."}]}, {"pnum": 57, "paragraph_text": "Back matter", "sentences": [{"sid": "S177", "text": "Back mat